Jakob Creutzig  <creutzig@mathematik.tu-darmstadt.de> wrote:
> On the other hand, it might be true that the human body is capable
> of creating more impulse when throwing heavy objects.

I'd guess so, because you're limited by how far and how fast you can
move your arm during the throwing action. If you throw a heavy suit
of armour, you're accelerating it as hard as you can for the time it
takes to travel a distance of (say) a metre. But try to give the
same amount of momentum to a light wand, and you'll find that to do
so with the limited acceleration you're able to provide, you'd have
to continue accelerating the wand over a distance much greater than
your arm can span during a throw.

(Also, when you're throwing a heavy object, most of your effort goes
into moving the object; but the lighter the object, the higher a
proportion of your effort is taken up by moving your arm.)

If you `threw' an object by hanging it in the air in front of you
and smacking it sharply with a single sharp blow, _then_ I might
believe that the impulse you imparted was roughly equivalent for all
kinds of object. However, a crystal plate mail would shatter into a
thousand pieces :-)
